Line 132 — R4 block, ttl 0 manual:

```js
m.resolvers[0][0]({ status: 200, body: "shared" });
assert.deepEqual(await p1, ...);  // p1's get: perform → fetchImpl(key).then(store handler)
```

Wait, line 132 is `await m.c.get("http://x/a")` — the "next call starts fresh" part. This is the last get, which starts a new fetch (manual → a new pending promise is pushed to resolvers). We're awaiting it, but we never resolve resolvers[1]! Bug in the test: need to resolve resolvers[1][0](...) first.

Let's look at that block:

```js
const m = mk({ ttlMs: 0, manual: true });
const p1 = m.c.get("http://x/a#1");
const p2 = m.c.get("http://x/a");
const p3 = m.c.get("http://x/a#2");
assert.equal(m.calls.length, 1);
m.resolvers[0][0]({ status: 200, body: "shared" });
assert.deepEqual(await p1, { status: 200, body: "shared" });
assert.deepEqual(await p2, { status: 200, body: "shared" });
assert.deepEqual(await p3, { status: 200, body: "shared" });
assert.deepEqual(m.c.stats(), { hits: 0, misses: 3, fetches: 1 });
// After completion, the next call starts fresh
m.setNow(5);
await m.c.get("http://x/a");   // <- line 132: new fetch, resolvers[1] is pending, we never resolve it!
assert.equal(m.calls.length, 2);
```

Fix: resolve resolvers[1] before/after:
```js
m.setNow(5);
const p4 = m.c.get("http://x/a");
m.resolvers[1][0]({ status: 200, body: "again" });
assert.deepEqual(await p4, { status: 200, body: "again" });
assert.equal(m.calls.length, 2);
```

